#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "996f01f7-cd57-5bdf-9d86-1357f25e9a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190526} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd0a928-02ff-504b-af24-ba60ae3b93c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(\\ln (5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190527} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c1f9e3-c3cd-5a53-8a99-03fe38e7926e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190528} {:answer/id #uuid "180e6c62-5333-5335-a9e5-4057e7fec682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(\\ln (10))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190529} {:answer/id #uuid "568ca47f-d076-5906-98d2-b6bb13b855ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2\\ln (5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd0a928-02ff-504b-af24-ba60ae3b93c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(\\ln (5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190527} :answer-field/id #uuid "68eb124c-c30f-59d0-8ca1-c05d8a184bff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190525}] :question/id #uuid "7611f3fb-7a78-5921-a935-9d3c3140eb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-152231" :question/problem "What is $∫_{- ∞}^{0}5^{x/2}dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afa44c40-cd6e-55f0-bd40-6a0c511ea31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326565} {:answer/id #uuid "5253cc7d-57b3-5804-9d16-bd5e8bd6d2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326566} {:answer/id #uuid "b98ff884-2527-5c3f-8422-a8be4a32dad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326567} {:answer/id #uuid "f94b0f81-d7bf-564c-a005-3246df866d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326568} {:answer/id #uuid "1290deab-c050-514a-9ec9-0b344ccf443f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1290deab-c050-514a-9ec9-0b344ccf443f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326569} :answer-field/id #uuid "28577854-a611-5098-b135-796f07c42fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b85f51a-1479-5d27-ba6d-960b7e7827ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-152232" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa485675-6e54-5354-a5be-95b88c6790a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aeedd54-c10a-53bd-b367-88f50ec08852" :question/math-academy-id "q-351320" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf178c6-ee17-560f-9c52-0b174654b01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326607} {:answer/id #uuid "6b63ef24-a739-56ad-b07f-5acb34cecd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b63ef24-a739-56ad-b07f-5acb34cecd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326608} :answer-field/id #uuid "719dc9c1-bc0e-5fc4-b08e-c41f599d575f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "875dd4ee-1f0c-53fd-8431-b98eef8a3afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-351528" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
